Brendan Guild wrote:
> I think a good start would be deciding that the locked doors would
> work like many locked doors in life: they would be freely openable
> from one side but locked from the other. That way you only have to
> ensure that there is a way to find the key on one side of the door,
> not both sides. If the player teleports past a door, it is just like
> getting a free key.

This is a quite interesting idea, although as I understood the YANI
referred to in this thread, keys are suggested here to work like
real-life keys and not video-game keys (that is, disappearing when used
once).

> On the other hand, you could surround locked rooms with walls that
> cannot be passed through by any means, including Xorns and teleports,
> and with ceilings that work similarly to prevent trapdoors. That
> would not substantially alter the game any more than the concept of
> locked doors intends to.

No, it would substantially change the game.  It would make polymorphing
into a Xorn and teleport control, which are fairly strong magic, less
useful.

There is an aspect of cheating in telling the player they can control
their teleports, and phase through walls, then turning around and
saying, effectively, except where I say you can't.  Also remember that
Nethack is famous for having many ways around its obstacles.  Making a
door a obstacle to anything *except* the right key is probably too
much.

This isn't to say the idea isn't meritless, just to suggest that you
don't need to worry that hard about making it harder to get past these
doors rather than easier.  Make them harder to bash down (maybe require
high strength) and that should be enough.

